::SPEECH_U02_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Speakers who rehearse with note cards can remember the order of their main points. Which paraphrase is most accurate?
{
~A. Speakers should never use note cards because they distract the audience.#This changes the meaning. The source says note cards can help speakers remember order.
~B. Main points are not important when a speaker rehearses.#This contradicts the source. The source connects note cards to remembering main points.
=C. Practicing with note cards can help speakers keep their main points in order.#Correct. This restates the source meaning in new wording without changing the idea.
~D. Note cards are only useful for writing essays.#This adds an unrelated idea and moves away from speech notes.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
Source: Clear transitions help listeners understand how one point connects to the next. What is the key meaning?
{
~A. Listeners do not need transitions.#This reverses the source meaning. The source says transitions help listeners.
~B. Speakers should avoid connecting points.#This is the opposite of the source idea.
~C. Transitions are only used in written essays.#The source is about listeners and speech points, not only essays.
=D. Transitions help the audience follow relationships between speech points.#Correct. This captures the important meaning of the source sentence.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which academic word should probably stay in a paraphrase about speech preparation?
{
=A. audience#Correct. Audience is a precise communication term and may be needed for accuracy.
~B. stuff#This is vague and not academic vocabulary.
~C. things#This is general and does not make the paraphrase precise.
~D. whatever#This is informal and not appropriate academic vocabulary.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: A concise speech note reminds the speaker of an idea without requiring a full sentence. Which speech note best paraphrases the idea?
{
~A. A concise speech note reminds the speaker of an idea without requiring a full sentence.#This copies the source exactly, so it is not a paraphrase.
=B. Short note: remember idea, do not read full sentence.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and changes it into a usable speech note.
~C. Full sentences are always required on note cards.#This changes the meaning because the source says a full sentence is not required.
~D. Notes should include every word of the speech.#This contradicts the source and would not support natural speaking.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which action is part of strong paraphrasing?
{
~A. Replace one word at a time and keep the same sentence structure.#This is too close to copying and does not show full paraphrasing.
~B. Add a new opinion that was not in the source.#This changes the source meaning and makes the paraphrase inaccurate.
=C. Understand the meaning, then restate it with new wording and structure.#Correct. Strong paraphrasing begins with meaning and then restates accurately.
~D. Remove the main idea so the sentence is shorter.#Shorter is not better if the important meaning is lost.
}
